# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files — never committed. Real values live only in .env.local and in the
# Vercel dashboard (doc 16 §8).
.env*
# …except the template, which holds names and comments but never a value.
!.env.example

# ⚠️ THE LEADING DOT IS NOT OPTIONAL, AND THAT IS WHY THIS SECOND RULE EXISTS.
# `.env*` matches `.env.local`. It does NOT match `env.download`,
# `env.local.old-project-backup` or `env.example` — no leading dot, no match.
# Three such files were sitting in this directory on 2026-08-23, two of them
# holding the live DATABASE_URL with its password, SESSION_SECRET,
# MFA_ENCRYPTION_KEY, TOKEN_PEPPER and RESEND_API_KEY. This repository is
# public. They were moved out of the working tree; this rule is what stops the
# next copy from being committed.
#
# Deliberately broad — a stray `env.backup` costs nothing to ignore, and a
# leaked key cannot be un-leaked by deleting the commit. It has to be rotated.
env*
!env.example.template

# Generated by the email preview run — a rendering of the templates, not source.
email-preview.html

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# SECRETS THAT ARRIVE AS FILES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Printing the recovery codes from /setup saves a PDF into whatever directory
# the browser last used — which, when the CRM is the thing you are working on,
# is this one. A set of Super Admin recovery codes was committed and pushed this
# way before anybody noticed.
#
# They are the ONLY way back into an account nobody else can reset, so they are
# exactly the thing that must never be in version control. Broad patterns on
# purpose: a near-miss here costs far more than an over-eager ignore rule.
*.pdf
# Report layout previews, written by `REPORT_POSTER_DUMP=1 npx vitest run lib/pdf`.
# ⚠️ Real project figures end up in these when the integration suite writes them.
.report-preview/
*recovery*code*
*recovery-codes*
*.p12
*.pfx
*.pem
*.key
secrets/

.vercel
.env*

# CLI deploy transcript, written by scripts and by hand. Not source.
deploy.log
